<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The dependency-inversion centre (ADR-0065, design 01 section 3.1).

    THIS PROJECT DEPENDS ON NOTHING, and that is a rule rather than a current
    state: design 01 section 3.1 line 97 says "`Abstractions` depends on
    nothing". There is no ProjectReference, and there is not meant to be one. An
    architecture test asserts it from the outside as of the test project's
    arrival, because a rule that only lives in a comment is a rule that gets
    edited away by whoever needs the reference.

    Until 2026-08-02 the sentence above also said there was no PackageReference.
    There is one now, and the rule is unchanged, because the rule is about what
    a CONSUMER inherits and an analyzer is not that. What makes that true is
    `PrivateAssets="all"`, which is load-bearing and was proven so rather than
    assumed. The analyzer's own nuspec declares `developmentDependency=true`,
    which reads like it settles the question and does not: packed WITHOUT
    PrivateAssets, `Nami.Identity.Abstractions.nuspec` carried
    `<dependency id="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" version="5.6.0"
    exclude="Build,Analyzers" />`, so every consumer restoring this package
    would have pulled it. With PrivateAssets the dependency group packs empty.
    Both nuspecs were read out of the built .nupkg, not inferred.

    So the architecture test named above must assert against the PACKED SURFACE
    or against compile-time references, not against the bare presence of a
    PackageReference item. The item is legitimately here and the rule still
    holds; a test that reads this file would fail on a correct project and would
    be "fixed" by deleting the analyzer.

    TargetFrameworks reads the knob rather than naming a framework, so a runtime
    bump touches Directory.Build.props once (ADR-0030 parameter C). The knob is
    two properties and not one on purpose: parameter B multi-targets libraries
    and single-targets the host, so one shared property would be wrong for one
    of them the day .NET 12 ships. Both read net10.0 today, which is exactly why
    the shape has to be right now rather than later, when the difference appears
    and the wrong shape is already load-bearing.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(NamiLibraryTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
